  "answer": "This looks like a known problem on Windows 10 (build 19045). File Explorer sometimes loses or never picks up the registration for Terminal's packaged context-menu entry. The Terminal team suspects this is an OS-side bug, so reinstalling Terminal doesn't fix it. Past tickets suggest the following:\n\n1. Restart Explorer without a full reboot. Run `taskkill /f /im explorer.exe && explorer.exe`. Explorer caches context-menu entries.\n2. Right-click an actual folder or file, not the empty background, the desktop or a drive root. On Windows 10 the entry often shows only for folders.\n3. If it's still missing, add the entry manually in the registry. Create `HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\\Directory\\background\\shell\\terminal\\command` with the default value `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Microsoft\\WindowsApps\\wt.exe -d \"%V\\.\"`. This was the workaround suggested by [user] in terminal#16236.\n4. If none of that works, send feedback through Feedback Hub under 'Desktop Environment > Right-click context menu' and share the link. The team asked for this so the Windows context-menu team can investigate.\n\nThe Windows 10 packaged-menu problem is tracked in #14185, and I didn't read that ticket."
 },
